<script>
const QS=[
{t:"Duty of care",q:"Following Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire [2018], what is the correct approach to determining whether a duty of care exists?",right:"Look first to established precedent and categories; use Caparo policy analysis only for genuinely novel cases where precedent gives no clear answer",wrong:["Apply the Caparo three-stage test in every duty of care scenario regardless of whether precedent already settles the position","Ask whether it is fair, just and reasonable on the facts as the primary analytical tool applied across all duty scenarios","Use the neighbour principle from Donoghue v Stevenson as the general overarching test applicable to all duty of care questions"],e:"Robinson confirmed that existing precedent is the starting point. Caparo is reserved for novel cases — it is not a universal formula applied every time."},
{t:"Duty of care",q:"D performs a positive act that foreseeably causes physical injury to C. What is the correct legal position?",right:"D owes C a duty of care automatically — an act foreseeably causing physical injury falls within an established category requiring no further policy analysis",wrong:["D owes C a duty of care only if proximity and fairness are additionally and independently established on the specific facts of the case","D may or may not owe a duty depending on policy considerations that the court must weigh carefully on the particular facts presented","D owes no duty of care unless C belongs to a defined and identifiable class within a pre-existing recognised legal relationship"],e:"An act which foreseeably causes physical injury or property damage automatically gives rise to a duty. No separate policy analysis is needed."},
{t:"Duty of care",q:"Which of the following is NOT a recognised form of 'actionable damage' required to complete a claim in negligence?",right:"Pure distress or upset without a medically recognised psychiatric condition diagnosable by clinical standards at the time of the claim",wrong:["Personal injury including physical harm to the body that goes beyond a trivial or de minimis threshold in any recognisable form","Property damage caused by the defendant's positive act rather than being pre-existing at the time of the negligent conduct","Economic loss directly consequent upon physical damage to property, rather than standing entirely alone as pure financial loss"],e:"Ordinary distress — however severe subjectively — is not actionable damage. A claimant must prove a recognised psychiatric illness to recover for psychiatric harm."},
{t:"Duty of care",q:"In Dryden v Johnson Matthey [2018], why did the Supreme Court hold that sensitisation to platinum salts constituted actionable damage?",right:"Sensitisation prevented the claimants from continuing in their jobs, making it a bodily change with real-world consequences amounting to personal injury",wrong:["Sensitisation was treated as property damage to the claimant's body rather than a form of recognised personal injury at common law","Sensitisation satisfied the Caparo three-stage test as it was foreseeable, proximate and fair to recognise as damage on those facts","Sensitisation qualified as pure economic loss because it directly caused the claimants financial losses through inability to work"],e:"The Supreme Court held sensitisation preventing employees from continuing work was personal injury — a bodily change with real consequences, not merely an abstract alteration."},
{t:"Breach of duty",q:"Which factor does NOT form part of the objective balancing exercise when assessing breach of duty in negligence?",right:"The defendant's subjective awareness of the particular risk of harm at the time of the alleged breach of their duty of care",wrong:["The probability that the defendant's conduct would cause harm to a person in the claimant's position in the circumstances","The gravity and potential seriousness of the harm that could result from the defendant's conduct on the facts presented","The cost, burden and practicability of taking precautions against the risk compared to the benefit that precautions would achieve"],e:"Breach is assessed objectively. The defendant's subjective awareness is irrelevant — the court asks what the reasonable person would have known and done in the circumstances."},
{t:"Breach of duty",q:"Under the Bolam test, a professional defendant is not in breach of their duty of care if they have:",right:"Acted in accordance with a practice accepted as proper by a responsible body of skilled professional opinion in that relevant field",wrong:["Acted in a way that at least one other fully qualified professional in the same field would have done in the same circumstances","Followed the most widely accepted or most commonly used practice among the majority of practitioners in their particular specialism","Acted in genuine good faith and believed at the time that their chosen course of treatment or professional action was correct"],e:"Bolam v Friern [1957]: a professional is not negligent if they acted in accordance with practice accepted by a responsible body of skilled opinion — even if a minority would disagree."},
{t:"Breach of duty",q:"Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health Board [2015] departed from the Bolam test specifically in relation to which medical context?",right:"Disclosure of material risks involved in a recommended treatment and the existence of reasonable alternative treatments to be discussed with the patient",wrong:["Selection and implementation of a particular surgical or procedural technique chosen by a clinician for a specific patient's ongoing care","Prescribing decisions involving selection of medication, dosage and administration for a patient with a known diagnosed medical condition","Diagnosis of medical conditions based on clinical interpretation of signs and symptoms presented by the patient at examination"],e:"Montgomery: the duty to warn of material risks is governed by a patient-centred standard, not Bolam. Patients must be informed of material risks and reasonable alternatives."},
{t:"Breach of duty",q:"A junior doctor is working in a senior registrar post and is sued for negligence arising from their clinical conduct. What standard of care applies?",right:"The standard expected of a reasonably competent person occupying the senior registrar post itself, regardless of their actual personal level of experience",wrong:["A reduced or modified standard that accounts for the defendant's genuine lack of experience and the early stage of their medical career at the time","The highest standard of care applicable to the most senior and specialised expert practitioner in that particular medical specialty","The standard of a first-year qualified doctor in training, appropriately adjusted for the specific context in which the breach occurred"],e:"Per Wilsher v Essex AHA [1987]: the standard is set by the post occupied, not the individual's experience. Inexperience is never a defence in professional negligence."},
{t:"Factual causation",q:"A patient dies from arsenic poisoning. The doctor is negligent but the evidence shows the patient would have died even with correct treatment. What is the outcome?",right:"The doctor is not liable — the but-for test is not satisfied because the breach made no difference to the outcome that would have occurred regardless",wrong:["The doctor is liable because they materially contributed to the risk of the patient's death by failing to provide timely and correct treatment","The doctor is partially liable for the pain and suffering the patient experienced in the period between the negligent act and inevitable death","The doctor is fully liable because they adopted the wrong course of action and thereby denied the patient any realistic prospect of survival"],e:"Barnett v Chelsea & Kensington Hospital [1969]: because the patient would have died regardless, the breach was not a but-for cause of death — no liability despite clear negligence."},
{t:"Factual causation",q:"Multiple employers negligently exposed C to asbestos causing mesothelioma — an indivisible injury. What does the law provide following Fairchild?",right:"Each employer who materially increased the risk of the disease is jointly and severally liable in full — a justified exception to standard but-for causation",wrong:["Each employer is liable only for their precise proportionate share of the total exposure period attributable to their particular workplace or employment","No employer is liable at all because standard but-for causation cannot be established on the balance of probabilities in mesothelioma cases","The employer responsible for the longest exposure period bears sole and exclusive liability for all of the claimant's losses and damage"],e:"Fairchild v Glenhaven [2002]: where each employer materially increased the risk of an indivisible disease, each is jointly and severally liable — a departure from strict but-for causation."},
{t:"Factual causation",q:"D's post-operative negligence weakens C; combined with C's pre-existing illness, C suffers brain damage neither cause alone could have produced. Which principle applies?",right:"Material contribution to an indivisible injury per Bailey v Ministry of Defence — D is fully liable because the negligence materially contributed to the outcome",wrong:["Loss of a chance — because it cannot be established with certainty whether the defendant's negligence was the truly decisive factor in causing harm","Material contribution to a divisible injury — making the defendant liable only for the precise proportion of harm attributable to their negligent conduct","The but-for test is straightforwardly satisfied on the balance of probabilities, making D fully liable on ordinary causation principles without special doctrine"],e:"Bailey v Ministry of Defence [2008]: where negligence combines with innocent causes to produce an indivisible injury, D is fully liable if their breach materially contributed."},
{t:"Factual causation",q:"In Hotson v East Berkshire AHA [1987], the claimant had a 75% pre-existing chance of avascular necrosis regardless of the defendant's conduct. Why did the claim fail?",right:"The claimant could not prove on the balance of probabilities that the negligent misdiagnosis caused the avascular necrosis — more likely it resulted from the original injury",wrong:["The claimant failed because the defendant owed no duty of care in respect of the specific type of harm that ultimately materialised on the facts","The claimant failed because the court confirmed that loss of a chance claims are categorically unavailable in personal injury cases under English law","The claimant failed because the damage was held to be too remote from the defendant's breach of duty to be recoverable as a matter of law"],e:"Hotson: C could not show on the balance of probabilities (>50%) that the misdiagnosis caused the injury — English law generally rejects 'loss of a chance' recovery for medical outcomes."},
{t:"Legal causation",q:"The thin skull (eggshell skull) rule in negligence law means that:",right:"Once a type of injury is foreseeable, D is fully liable for the actual extent of harm even where C's particular vulnerability makes the consequences far worse",wrong:["D is not liable for any injury that was entirely unforeseeable in type even where the claimant was known to have unusual vulnerability to physical harm","Damages are reduced proportionately where the claimant had a pre-existing medical condition that contributed independently to the severity of injuries suffered","D is only liable for the foreseeable and anticipated extent of injury, with any excess attributable entirely to the claimant's own pre-existing condition"],e:"Smith v Leech Brain [1962]: once the type of injury is foreseeable, D takes C as they find them — fully liable for the extent however unusual C's vulnerability makes the outcome."},
{t:"Legal causation",q:"The Wagon Mound (No 1) [1961] established which test for determining whether damage is too remote from the defendant's breach?",right:"A defendant is only liable for damage of a type that was reasonably foreseeable at the time of the breach, even if the precise manner was not anticipated",wrong:["A defendant is liable for all damage that is a direct and natural consequence of the breach, regardless of whether the particular type of harm was foreseeable","A defendant is only liable for damage of an identical kind to the harm specifically anticipated or contemplated by a reasonable person in their position","Damage must be proved on the balance of probabilities to have been caused by the breach rather than by any subsequent intervening event or cause"],e:"The Wagon Mound (No 1) replaced the 'direct consequence' test with remoteness: defendants are only liable for types of damage that were reasonably foreseeable at the time of breach."},
{t:"Legal causation",q:"Hughes v Lord Advocate [1963] established which principle about foreseeability and remoteness of damage?",right:"It is sufficient that the general type of harm was foreseeable — the precise manner or sequence in which it actually came about need not have been anticipated",wrong:["The precise manner and specific mechanism by which harm comes about must always be independently foreseeable for liability to properly attach to the defendant","Any harm with a factual causal connection to the defendant's breach is fully recoverable regardless of whether the type of harm was itself foreseeable","Physical injury is always regarded as sufficiently foreseeable that the remoteness doctrine has no practical limiting application in personal injury cases"],e:"Hughes v Lord Advocate: the precise sequence need not be foreseeable — it suffices that the general type of harm (fire/explosion) was within the range of foreseeable risk."},
{t:"Legal causation",q:"A third party's intervening act breaks the chain of causation between D's breach and C's damage only if:",right:"The third party's act is sufficiently independent, unforeseeable and unreasonable to make it unjust to hold D responsible for the consequences that follow from it",wrong:["The third party is a professional whose conduct fell below the standard expected of a competent practitioner in the relevant field at the relevant time","The third party acted deliberately and intentionally rather than negligently, thereby making their conduct the dominant and sole cause of the claimant's loss","D was not at all negligent in relation to the events or circumstances that created the opportunity for the third party to intervene as they did"],e:"A novus actus breaks the chain only if sufficiently independent and unforeseeable. A foreseeable negligent act — e.g. standard medical complications — generally will not sever the chain."},
{t:"Pure economic loss",q:"In Spartan Steel v Martin [1973], which category of loss was held to be pure economic loss and therefore NOT recoverable in negligence?",right:"Loss of profit on batches of steel that could have been manufactured during the remaining hours of the power cut but were never started or begun",wrong:["Physical damage to the molten steel already in the furnace at the precise moment when the power supply was cut off by the defendant's negligence","Loss of profit on the specific batch of steel that was physically damaged in the furnace and had to be poured away due to the power failure","Physical damage to the factory's furnace machinery and electrical equipment caused by the sudden and unexpected interruption in the power supply"],e:"Category (iii) — profit on steel never begun — was pure economic loss: not consequent on any physical damage, and therefore not recoverable in negligence."},
{t:"Pure economic loss",q:"Which landmark case first established that negligent misstatements causing pure economic loss can give rise to a duty of care in negligence?",right:"Hedley Byrne & Co v Heller and Partners [1964], recognising liability where there is assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance on a statement",wrong:["Donoghue v Stevenson [1932], which established the neighbour principle and laid the foundations for the modern tort of negligence as a whole","Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990], which formulated the three-stage test of foreseeability, proximity and fairness applied in novel duty cases","Murphy v Brentwood District Council [1991], which restricted recovery for economic loss arising from defective premises and overruled the Anns approach"],e:"Hedley Byrne established that a duty can arise for negligent misstatements causing pure economic loss where there is assumption of responsibility and reasonable reliance by the claimant."},
{t:"Pure economic loss",q:"For a Hedley Byrne assumption of responsibility claim to succeed, which element is NOT required?",right:"A formal contractual relationship or payment arrangement between the defendant and claimant at the time the advice or statement was given",wrong:["The defendant must have possessed special skill, expertise or relevant knowledge relating to the subject matter of the statement or advice","The defendant must have voluntarily assumed responsibility for the accuracy and reliability of the advice or statement provided to the claimant","The claimant must have actually and reasonably relied on the defendant's statement or advice when taking the relevant action or decision"],e:"No contract is required for Hedley Byrne liability. The doctrine applies to 'special relationships' short of contract — characterised by assumption of responsibility, special skill, and reasonable reliance."},
{t:"Pure economic loss",q:"In Caparo Industries v Dickman [1990], why did the House of Lords find the auditors owed no duty of care to investors who purchased shares?",right:"The audit was prepared for existing shareholders scrutinising management at the AGM — its purpose did not extend to potential investors making share purchase decisions",wrong:["The auditors had expressly disclaimed responsibility for any reliance on the audit report by parties other than the company that commissioned it","Investors could not demonstrate that they had actually read and specifically relied upon the audit report before making their investment decision","The audit report itself was not negligently prepared and therefore no breach of any duty that might otherwise have been owed could be established"],e:"No assumption of responsibility extended to the investing public. The audit's purpose was internal governance — not to inform strangers making investment decisions in the open market."},
{t:"Pure psychiatric injury",q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes primary victims from secondary victims in negligence claims for pure psychiatric injury?",right:"Primary victims are within the zone of physical danger or directly imperilled; secondary victims witness harm to others and must satisfy strict Alcock control criteria",wrong:["Primary victims are bystanders who observe events from a distance; secondary victims are those directly and physically involved in the incident itself","Primary victims must always prove a medically recognised psychiatric illness; secondary victims need only demonstrate that they suffered significant distress","Secondary victims are automatically and categorically excluded from any recovery for pure psychiatric injury regardless of their connection to the primary victim"],e:"Per Page v Smith [1996] and Alcock [1992]: primary victims are directly imperilled; secondary victims — witnessing harm to others — must satisfy the strict Alcock control mechanisms."},
{t:"Pure psychiatric injury",q:"The three control mechanisms for secondary victims in psychiatric injury claims were established in Alcock v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire [1992]. They are:",right:"Close tie of love and affection with the primary victim, proximity in time and space to the shocking event, and perception through their own unaided senses",wrong:["Reasonable foreseeability of psychiatric injury, close legal or familial proximity to the defendant, and a pre-existing relationship with the person causing harm","Actual presence at the scene of the accident, physical injury to the secondary victim themselves, and a medically confirmed diagnosed psychiatric condition","Reasonable foreseeability of nervous shock, a formal recognised legal relationship with the primary victim, and direct financial dependency on that person"],e:"Alcock's three control mechanisms: (1) close tie of love and affection, (2) proximity in time and space to the event, and (3) perception through their own unaided senses — not via broadcast media."},
{t:"Pure psychiatric injury",q:"Following White v Chief Constable of South Yorkshire [1999], rescuers are treated as primary victims only where:",right:"The rescuer was themselves within the zone of physical danger and could reasonably have apprehended a risk of physical injury to their own person",wrong:["The rescuer is acting in any capacity that could be described as rescuing another person regardless of their own exposure to any physical danger","The rescuer is a professional emergency worker such as a police officer, paramedic or firefighter acting in the course of their professional duties","The rescuer directly witnesses the death or very serious injury of another person as a proximate result of the defendant's negligent conduct"],e:"White: police at Hillsborough not in physical danger were treated as secondary victims and, failing Alcock criteria, could not recover under the rescuer category."},
{t:"Pure omissions",q:"What is the general rule concerning liability for pure omissions in English negligence law?",right:"There is no general duty to rescue or prevent harm through pure omission — English law does not impose liability merely for failing to act to assist a stranger",wrong:["There is a general duty to take positive steps to prevent foreseeable harm to others whenever doing so would not impose an unreasonable burden on the defendant","Omissions are treated in exactly the same way as positive acts for all purposes of establishing a duty of care in claims for negligence","A duty to take positive action arises automatically whenever harm to another person is reasonably foreseeable and prevention was practically achievable"],e:"English law imposes no general duty to rescue strangers. As confirmed in Smith v Littlewoods [1987] and N v Poole BC [2019], pure omissions do not generate liability without a special relationship."},
{t:"Pure omissions",q:"N v Poole Borough Council [2019] confirmed that a public authority owes a positive duty to act to protect a claimant only where:",right:"There was a specific assumption of responsibility for the claimant's welfare, or the authority itself created or materially increased the danger the claimant faced",wrong:["The harm to the claimant was reasonably foreseeable and the authority had sufficient resources and practical capacity to take steps to prevent it","The claimant was a particularly vulnerable person and the authority was made fully aware of that vulnerability through their professional role or function","The Caparo three-stage test of foreseeability, proximity and fairness is independently and cumulatively satisfied on all the facts of the particular case"],e:"N v Poole BC: public authorities owe no general protective duty against third-party harm unless they assumed responsibility for the claimant or created/increased the danger themselves."},
{t:"Intentional torts",q:"Which statement correctly identifies all essential elements of the common law tort of battery?",right:"An intentional and direct application of unlawful force to another person, which is actionable per se without any need to prove actual loss or damage",wrong:["A negligent infliction of physical harm that causes measurable and quantifiable damage to the claimant's body or their physical property","An intentional act that causes the claimant to reasonably apprehend the immediate infliction of unlawful physical force upon their own person","A deliberate course of harassing conduct that causes a recognised psychiatric illness to the claimant through repeated targeted behaviour"],e:"Battery = intentional, direct application of unlawful force. Actionable per se — no damage need be proved. The intent goes to the act itself, not necessarily to causing harm to the claimant."},
{t:"Intentional torts",q:"Thomas says quietly to Henry 'I'll get you for this.' Henry feels no particular alarm. Has the tort of assault been committed on these facts?",right:"No — there is no reasonable apprehension of immediate unlawful force; the words lack the immediacy that the tort of assault requires to be established",wrong:["Yes — any threatening words directed at another person are automatically sufficient to constitute the common law tort of assault in all circumstances","Yes — the defendant's subjective intention to cause alarm is sufficient regardless of whether Henry actually experienced fear or apprehension","No — Thomas did not physically touch or make any direct physical contact with Henry at any relevant point during the incident"],e:"Assault requires reasonable apprehension of immediate unlawful force. 'I'll get you' without immediacy or accompanying threatening conduct that creates immediate apprehension is insufficient."},
{t:"Intentional torts",q:"Which statement about the tort of false imprisonment is correct as a matter of English common law?",right:"False imprisonment requires total restraint of the claimant's freedom of movement — partial obstruction that leaves movement possible in some directions is insufficient",wrong:["False imprisonment requires the claimant to have been physically aware and consciously conscious of the restraint at the time it was actually imposed","False imprisonment requires proof of actual damage, distinguishing it clearly from battery and assault which are both actionable without proof of loss","False imprisonment can only be committed by or directly on behalf of a public authority exercising some form of official governmental or police power"],e:"False imprisonment = total restraint of C's freedom of movement from a particular place. Per Bird v Jones [1845]: partial obstruction allowing some movement is not false imprisonment."},
{t:"Intentional torts",q:"What is the key distinction between the tort in Wilkinson v Downton [1897] and negligence-based claims for psychiatric injury?",right:"Wilkinson v Downton requires intentional or reckless conduct calculated to cause harm; negligence requires only careless conduct falling below the reasonable standard",wrong:["Wilkinson v Downton requires proof of a recognised psychiatric illness as a necessary element of the cause of action while negligence-based claims do not","Wilkinson v Downton applies only to cases involving omissions and failures to act while negligence covers only positive acts causing direct physical harm","There is no meaningful practical distinction between the two causes of action and they are effectively interchangeable in contemporary litigation"],e:"Wilkinson v Downton covers intentional or reckless conduct calculated to cause physical or psychiatric harm — filling gaps left by the trespass torts where conduct is deliberate but not directly physical."},
{t:"Occupiers' liability",q:"Under the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, to whom is the statutory 'common duty of care' owed by the occupier of premises?",right:"All lawful visitors — those who enter with the occupier's express or implied permission, or who have a legal right of entry conferred by law",wrong:["All persons who physically enter the premises regardless of whether they have permission or any legal entitlement to enter or be present there","Lawful visitors and trespassers alike, with the same standard of care being owed to all persons regardless of the basis on which they entered","Only paying entrants who have provided consideration to the occupier in exchange for a contractual right to access and use the premises"],e:"The OLA 1957 applies only to lawful visitors — those with express or implied permission or a legal right of entry. Trespassers are governed by the separate OLA 1984."},
{t:"Occupiers' liability",q:"Under s.2(4)(b) OLA 1957, how may an occupier discharge their duty of care in relation to work carried out by an independent contractor on the premises?",right:"By taking reasonable steps to check the contractor is competent and, where the work is technical in nature, that it has actually been properly carried out",wrong:["The occupier's duty is automatically discharged the moment any independent contractor is engaged — all liability transfers entirely to the contractor","By displaying a prominent notice at the entrance to the premises clearly excluding liability for the contractor's negligent workmanship or omissions","By personally supervising every aspect of the contractor's work throughout the entire duration of the project to prevent any errors arising"],e:"s.2(4)(b) OLA 1957: the occupier may discharge their duty by taking reasonable steps to check the contractor's competence and, for technical work, that it has been properly executed."},
{t:"Occupiers' liability",q:"Under s.1(3) Occupiers' Liability Act 1984, when does an occupier owe a duty of care to a trespasser?",right:"Where the occupier knew of the danger, knew trespassers might be in the vicinity, and the risk was one against which it was reasonable to offer some protection",wrong:["Where the trespasser was a child; the occupier intentionally created the danger; and the danger was obvious and clearly visible to a reasonable adult","Where the danger was severe enough to cause serious injury; the occupier was insured against the risk; and the trespasser was not acting unlawfully","Where the trespasser suffered actual damage; the occupier had previously warned others; and the risk was foreseeable to a reasonable occupier"],e:"s.1(3) OLA 1984: awareness of the danger, awareness that trespassers might encounter it, and that offering some protection is reasonable in all the circumstances."},
{t:"Occupiers' liability",q:"Under s.2(4)(a) Occupiers' Liability Act 1957, when does a warning sign discharge an occupier's common duty of care to a lawful visitor?",right:"Only when the warning is sufficient in its terms to enable the visitor to take adequate steps to be reasonably safe and avoid the relevant danger",wrong:["Whenever any warning is displayed in any form, however inadequate or unclear, provided it is placed somewhere visible on the occupier's premises","Only when the visitor has physically read and demonstrably understood the specific content of the warning sign before the relevant accident occurred","Never — a warning alone is always insufficient and must be accompanied by physical barriers or other protective measures to discharge the duty"],e:"s.2(4)(a): a warning only discharges the duty if sufficient to enable the visitor to be reasonably safe. A vague warning that does not enable avoidance of the risk will not suffice."},
{t:"Defamation",q:"Under s.1 Defamation Act 2013, a statement is only actionable as defamatory if it meets which threshold requirement?",right:"It has caused, or is likely to cause, serious harm to the claimant's reputation — mere embarrassment, trivial hurt feelings or minor reputational damage is insufficient",wrong:["It causes the claimant significant emotional distress or psychological harm of a kind that any reasonable person would find seriously upsetting in the circumstances","It is published to the claimant's employer or to any person who holds professional authority over the claimant's career or business interests","It is published to more than a minimal number of recipients and is clearly understood by them to refer to the claimant in a defamatory sense"],e:"s.1 DA 2013 introduced the 'serious harm' threshold. Mere embarrassment or trivial reputational damage is insufficient — there must be serious harm (or serious financial loss for trading bodies)."},
{t:"Defamation",q:"The statutory defence of truth under s.2 Defamation Act 2013 succeeds where the defendant establishes that:",right:"The defamatory imputation conveyed by the statement is substantially true in all its material respects on the balance of probabilities",wrong:["The defendant published the statement in good faith and genuinely believed the content to be true at the time of publication to the relevant audience","The statement was on a matter of significant public interest and the defendant followed responsible journalistic standards in verifying the content","The defendant can show that the statement was true in at least some of its allegations even if other specific parts remain unverified or contested"],e:"Truth (formerly 'justification'): requires proof that the imputation conveyed is substantially true — not merely that the defendant believed it or that some allegations were accurate."},
{t:"Defamation",q:"For the defence of honest comment in defamation to succeed, which element is NOT required to be established?",right:"That the comment is one a reasonable and informed person would themselves agree with or find defensible as an expression of opinion on the relevant matter",wrong:["That the comment is on a matter of genuine public interest rather than a purely private matter of no legitimate concern to the wider public","That the comment is based on facts that are themselves true or that are protected by privilege in the relevant legal circumstances","That the comment genuinely reflects an opinion that the defendant honestly holds, however extreme or controversial that opinion may be"],e:"Honest comment does not require objective reasonableness — the question is whether the defendant honestly holds the opinion. However extreme, it suffices if genuine opinion on true facts on a public interest matter."},
{t:"Defamation",q:"The defence of absolute privilege in defamation law protects which category of statements?",right:"Statements made during parliamentary proceedings and in the course of judicial proceedings, regardless of whether they were made with malice or bad faith",wrong:["Any statement published by a responsible journalist on a matter of genuine public concern following reasonable verification of the underlying facts","Statements made during academic peer review or scientific publication where the defendant acted without any dishonest or improper motive throughout","Any statement made without actual malice by a person who honestly believed the statement to be true at the time of its publication or communication"],e:"Absolute privilege provides a complete, irrebuttable defence — covering Parliament (Bill of Rights 1689) and judicial proceedings, regardless of malice. It is the strongest form of privilege available."},
{t:"Product liability",q:"Under s.3 Consumer Protection Act 1987, a product is 'defective' when:",right:"Its safety is not such as persons generally are entitled to expect, assessed in light of all relevant circumstances including marketing, instructions and anticipated use",wrong:["It fails to perform in the precise way or to the exact standard that the manufacturer intended or stated in their published promotional materials","It causes any kind of physical damage or personal injury regardless of whether that damage arose from normal or abnormal use of the product","It does not comply with the specific product description, specifications or representations made by the defendant in advertising or marketing materials"],e:"s.3 CPA 1987: a product is defective if not as safe as persons generally are entitled to expect — an objective standard assessed against all relevant circumstances including purpose and instructions."},
{t:"Product liability",q:"Which of the following is a complete statutory defence for a producer under s.4 Consumer Protection Act 1987?",right:"The producer proves the defect did not exist in the product when put into circulation, or that scientific knowledge at the time meant the defect could not have been discovered",wrong:["The producer took all reasonable precautions and exercised all due diligence to prevent the defect from arising at any stage of the production process","The product fully complied with all applicable regulatory requirements and mandatory safety standards that were in force at the time of its manufacture","The claimant misused or modified the product in a manner that was not reasonably foreseeable and that independently contributed to the damage suffered"],e:"s.4(1)(b) CPA 1987: the defect-at-circulation defence is complete. The development risks defence (s.4(1)(e)) is also available where the defect was scientifically undiscoverable at the time."},
{t:"Product liability",q:"Which type of loss is expressly excluded from recovery under the Consumer Protection Act 1987 statutory scheme?",right:"Pure economic loss such as lost business profits, loss of revenue or financial loss that is not consequent upon any physical damage or personal injury",wrong:["Personal injury or death caused by the defective product where the defect arose during the manufacturing or production process itself","Damage to property ordinarily intended for and used for private purposes, where the damage caused exceeds the statutory minimum threshold of £275","Damage to property belonging to the claimant that was caused by the defective product acting destructively on other property in their possession"],e:"The CPA 1987 covers death, personal injury, and damage to other private property (above £275). Pure economic loss and damage to the defective product itself are excluded from the scheme."},
{t:"Trespass to land",q:"Which statement correctly identifies the essential legal characteristics of the tort of trespass to land?",right:"A direct and intentional interference with land in another's possession, which is actionable per se without any requirement to prove actual loss or damage",wrong:["An indirect and unreasonable interference with the claimant's use and enjoyment of their land caused by the defendant's activities on neighbouring land","A negligent act by the defendant that causes quantifiable physical damage to the property in the possession or ownership of the claimant","Any interference with land belonging to another person, regardless of the defendant's intention, provided that actual quantifiable damage can be demonstrated"],e:"Trespass to land = direct, intentional interference with land in another's possession. Actionable per se — no damage need be proved. Negligent or indirect interferences sound in nuisance, not trespass."},
{t:"Trespass to land",q:"D genuinely but mistakenly believes the land they walk across belongs to them. Is the tort of trespass to land established on these facts?",right:"Yes — the only intention required is the intention to make the physical entry; mistake as to title or ownership of the land is not a defence to trespass",wrong:["No — trespass requires the defendant to have actual knowledge that the land belongs to another person and to have deliberately entered regardless of that knowledge","No — the defendant lacked the necessary intention to trespass because they genuinely and honestly believed they had a legal right to enter the land","Yes — but the defendant's genuine mistake is a factor the court may properly take into account when assessing the quantum of any damages awarded"],e:"The intent required is only the intent to make the physical entry. Mistake as to title provides no defence — intentional entry onto another's land completes the tort of trespass."},
{t:"Trespass to land",q:"Which of the following constitutes trespass to land rather than being actionable (if at all) in private nuisance?",right:"D deliberately throws objects over a fence so that they physically land on and remain on C's land without C's permission or authorisation",wrong:["Smoke and noxious fumes from D's factory chimney regularly drift across the boundary and deposit particles on C's adjoining residential property","Noise and vibrations generated by D's operations on their own land cause persistent disturbance and loss of quiet enjoyment to C on C's land","Water naturally draining from D's higher ground flows onto C's lower-lying adjoining land and accumulates causing dampness and structural damage"],e:"Trespass requires direct physical interference — throwing objects onto C's land qualifies. Smoke, noise, and natural water flow are indirect interferences, actionable if at all in nuisance."},
{t:"Private nuisance",q:"What proprietary standing must a claimant have to bring a claim in private nuisance following Hunter v Canary Wharf [1997]?",right:"A proprietary or possessory interest in the land affected — persons who merely occupy land without such a legal interest have no standing to sue in nuisance",wrong:["No proprietary interest is required — any person who suffers personal injury or significant distress as a result of the nuisance may bring a civil claim","A contractual licence to occupy the premises, even without any formal proprietary interest in the land itself, is sufficient to confer the necessary standing","Only freehold owners of the affected property have standing — leasehold tenants and other limited interest holders are entirely excluded from claiming"],e:"Per Hunter v Canary Wharf [1997]: only those with a proprietary or possessory interest have standing. Family members and licensees without such an interest cannot claim in private nuisance."},
{t:"Private nuisance",q:"Which factor is LEAST relevant when assessing whether an interference constitutes an unreasonable interference for purposes of private nuisance?",right:"The defendant's subjective belief that their activities are not causing harm or any unreasonable interference to neighbouring occupiers of adjacent land",wrong:["The character and nature of the neighbourhood in which both the defendant's and claimant's properties are respectively situated and being used","The duration, frequency and persistence of the alleged interference as an objectively relevant factor in the overall reasonableness assessment","Whether the claimant's use of their own land is abnormally sensitive compared to what a typical and ordinary occupier of similar property might expect"],e:"The test in private nuisance is objective. A defendant's subjective belief that they cause no harm is irrelevant. Locality, duration, and abnormal sensitivity are all objectively relevant considerations."},
{t:"Private nuisance",q:"Cambridge Water Company v Eastern Counties Leather [1994] confirmed which requirement applies in both private nuisance and Rylands v Fletcher?",right:"Foreseeability of the type of damage suffered is a necessary prerequisite — defendants are not liable for wholly unforeseeable consequences of their activities on land",wrong:["Liability is strict and absolute in both private nuisance and Rylands v Fletcher entirely regardless of whether the type of damage was foreseeable","Only physical damage to tangible property is recoverable in private nuisance — amenity interference and loss of enjoyment are entirely excluded from the remedy","Foreseeability of damage is not required at all provided that the defendant's use of their land was demonstrably unreasonable in the relevant circumstances"],e:"Cambridge Water confirmed that foreseeability of the type of damage is required even in nuisance and Rylands v Fletcher — these are not cases of absolute liability for all unforeseeable consequences."},
{t:"Public nuisance",q:"What is the key distinction between the tort of public nuisance and the tort of private nuisance as actionable civil claims?",right:"Public nuisance affects the rights of the public generally or a class of the public; private nuisance concerns an individual's use and enjoyment of their own land",wrong:["Public nuisance applies only to noise, vibration and atmospheric pollution; private nuisance applies to all other types of interference with land use","Public nuisance always requires proof of actual physical damage to bring a civil claim; private nuisance is actionable without any proof of damage","Private nuisance is both a civil tort and a criminal offence; public nuisance is a civil action only without any corresponding criminal law liability"],e:"Public nuisance affects the rights of the public generally or a class of people. Private nuisance is about an individual's enjoyment of their land. Public nuisance is also a crime."},
{t:"Public nuisance",q:"To bring a private civil claim for damages in public nuisance, an individual claimant must prove they have suffered:",right:"'Particular' or 'special' damage — harm that is over and above the damage suffered by other members of the general public affected by the nuisance",wrong:["A proprietary or possessory interest in land situated in the vicinity of the nuisance, establishing their necessary standing to bring a civil claim","Loss of a public right of way or other public right that was previously enjoyed by the claimant and others in the affected area before the nuisance arose","That the defendant acted intentionally or with reckless disregard in creating or maintaining the state of affairs constituting the public nuisance"],e:"To bring a private civil action in public nuisance, the individual must prove 'particular' or 'special' damage — something over and above what the rest of the affected public suffered."},
{t:"Employers' liability",q:"In Lister v Hesley Hall [2001], why did the House of Lords find the employer vicariously liable for a warden's sexual abuse of residents?",right:"There was a sufficiently close connection between the employee's role as warden — with responsibility for the boys — and the torts committed against them",wrong:["The employer had expressly authorised the employee to engage in the relevant conduct as part of his formally defined duties at the residential institution","The employer was independently negligent in their own right by failing to conduct adequate employment checks before appointing the employee","The employee was performing a non-delegable duty on behalf of the employer which could not in law be discharged through or delegated to another person"],e:"Lister introduced the 'close connection' test: the employer is vicariously liable where the employee's tort is so closely connected to their employment that it is just to hold the employer responsible."},
{t:"Employers' liability",q:"Lord Sumption's five-factor test for identifying when a non-delegable duty arises was articulated in which leading case?",right:"Woodland v Swimming Teachers Association [2014], where factors include vulnerability, a pre-existing protective relationship, and delegation of a function to a third party",wrong:["Wilson and Clyde Coal v English [1938], which first established the employer's primary personal duty to provide safe plant, premises and work systems","Lister v Hesley Hall [2001], which introduced the close connection test for vicarious liability in cases involving intentional wrongdoing by employees","Various Claimants v Catholic Child Welfare Society [2012], which extended vicarious liability to encompass relationships that are merely akin to employment"],e:"Lord Sumption in Woodland [2014] identified five factors for a non-delegable duty: vulnerability, pre-existing protective relationship, delegation to a third party, that party's negligence, and injury."},
{t:"Employers' liability",q:"For vicarious liability to arise, the employee's tort must have been committed in circumstances where:",right:"Their conduct is sufficiently closely connected to their employment role for it to be just and reasonable to hold the employer vicariously liable for the tort",wrong:["The employer gave express instructions and the employee acted within the precise scope of the tasks they were specifically and formally authorised to perform","The tort occurred during the employee's contracted working hours and on the employer's premises or other workplace specified in their employment contract","The employer had actual advance knowledge of the specific risk that the relevant type of harm might occur to the particular claimant involved"],e:"Vicarious liability requires the tort to be committed in the course of employment — sufficiently closely connected to the employee's role (the Lister/Mohamud 'close connection' test)."},
{t:"Employers' liability",q:"Following Various Claimants v Catholic Child Welfare Society [2012], vicarious liability may arise beyond formal employment where the relationship is:",right:"'Akin to employment' — where the defendant created the risk by placing the tortfeasor in their position and derives benefit from their activities or enterprise",wrong:["Governed by a written service agreement specifying the tasks to be performed and the standards of performance expected of the service provider","Subject to the defendant's direct day-to-day control over the precise manner in which the tortfeasor carries out their work and all activities","Established by a formal contract of employment conferring all associated statutory employment rights and obligations that apply in law"],e:"Various Claimants: vicarious liability extends to relationships 'akin to employment' where the defendant created the risk, derives benefit, and has the ability to control or direct the tortfeasor."},
{t:"Defences",q:"The defence of volenti non fit injuria requires the defendant to establish that the claimant:",right:"Both knew of the specific risk of harm and freely and voluntarily agreed to accept that legal risk — mere knowledge of the risk alone is not sufficient",wrong:["Merely knew of the general existence of some risk in the relevant activity, making any resulting harm entirely their own legal and moral responsibility","Was contributorily negligent in their own conduct, thereby assuming partial legal responsibility for some portion of the damage they ultimately suffered","Signed a written waiver, contractual disclaimer or formal exclusion of liability before engaging in the relevant risky activity with the defendant"],e:"Volenti requires both knowledge of the risk and free, voluntary acceptance of the legal risk. Mere knowledge (scienti) alone is insufficient — there must be a genuine uncoerced choice to accept the risk."},
{t:"Defences",q:"Contributory negligence under the Law Reform (Contributory Negligence) Act 1945 operates as:",right:"A partial defence that reduces the claimant's damages in proportion to their share of responsibility, considering both comparative fault and causal contribution",wrong:["A complete defence that entirely defeats the claimant's claim where their own negligent conduct materially contributed to the harm they suffered","A defence that transfers liability entirely from the defendant to the claimant wherever both parties are found to be equally at fault for an accident","A procedural bar preventing the claimant from bringing any claim where they were engaged in any form of unlawful or inherently dangerous activity"],e:"Under the 1945 Act, contributory negligence is partial — damages are reduced to reflect C's share of fault, considering both the degree of blameworthiness and causal potency of each party's conduct."},
{t:"Defences",q:"After Patel v Mirza [2016], courts considering the illegality defence (ex turpi causa) must conduct:",right:"A multi-factor balancing exercise weighing the purpose of the rule breached, coherence of the legal system, and whether denying the claim would be disproportionate",wrong:["A strict categorical approach under which any criminal conduct by the claimant automatically and entirely bars their negligence claim as a matter of legal principle","An assessment limited exclusively to whether the claimant was formally convicted of a criminal offence arising directly from the relevant conduct in question","A pure factual causation analysis asking only whether the claimant's own unlawful conduct was the proximate and dominant cause of their own damage"],e:"Patel v Mirza rejected rigidity in favour of a multi-factor analysis: courts weigh the purpose of the rule breached, other relevant policies, and whether denying the claim is disproportionate."},
{t:"Defences",q:"To which of the following torts does the defence of contributory negligence NOT apply?",right:"Battery and other intentional torts — as confirmed by Alliance & Leicester Building Society v Edgestop Ltd [1993] as a matter of settled English law",wrong:["Negligence claims arising from road traffic accidents or personal injury caused by careless driving on a public road or highway in England","Private nuisance claims where the claimant's own use of their land independently contributed to the severity of the interference they complain of","Occupiers' liability claims brought under either the Occupiers' Liability Act 1957 or the Occupiers' Liability Act 1984 as applicable"],e:"Per Alliance & Leicester v Edgestop [1993]: contributory negligence does not apply to intentional torts such as battery. It remains available for negligence, nuisance, and occupiers' liability claims."},
{t:"Remedies",q:"The primary aim of compensatory damages in tort law is to:",right:"Restore the claimant to the position they would have been in had the tort not been committed — the restitutionary principle established in Livingstone v Rawyards Coal Co",wrong:["Punish the defendant for their wrongdoing and provide a general deterrent against similar conduct by the defendant and other members of society","Vindicate the claimant's legal rights publicly by making a judicial declaration that those rights have been infringed by the defendant's conduct","Reflect the moral wrongfulness of the defendant's conduct by awarding a sum proportionate to the overall seriousness of the breach of duty"],e:"Compensatory damages aim to restore C to the position they would have been in but for the tort — per Livingstone v Rawyards Coal Co [1880]. They are restitutionary in aim, not punitive."},
{t:"Remedies",q:"Under the Rookes v Barnard [1964] categories, in which circumstances may exemplary (punitive) damages be awarded?",right:"Where D's conduct was calculated to produce a profit exceeding any compensation payable, or involved oppressive government action, or where statute authorises them",wrong:["Whenever the claimant suffered particularly serious or life-altering harm as a direct result of the defendant's negligent or intentional wrongdoing in any form","Whenever the defendant acted with gross negligence amounting to reckless disregard for the safety or legal rights of the claimant in question","Whenever the defendant acted intentionally in any form, making exemplary damages broadly available across the full range of intentional tort claims"],e:"Rookes v Barnard: exemplary damages are available only in two non-statutory categories — (1) oppressive government conduct and (2) calculated profit-seeking — and where statute permits. They remain exceptional."},
{t:"Remedies",q:"A prohibitory injunction in tort law operates by:",right:"Restraining the defendant from continuing or repeating their wrongful conduct — it is an equitable remedy granted at the court's discretion and not as of right",wrong:["Ordering the defendant to take positive and remedial steps to undo or remedy the consequences of the wrongful conduct they have already carried out","Providing the claimant with a lump sum of money representing the estimated future cost of preventing the relevant harm from recurring on their property","Automatically transferring legal ownership of the wrongfully used property from the defendant to the claimant as an appropriate remedy in kind"],e:"A prohibitory injunction restrains future wrongful conduct (e.g. stopping a nuisance). A mandatory injunction orders positive action. Both are equitable — granted at the court's discretion, not as of right."},
{t:"Remedies",q:"Following a death caused by the defendant's tort, who may bring claims for compensation and under which legislative framework?",right:"Dependants may claim under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976 for loss of dependency; separately the estate may bring surviving claims under the Law Reform Act 1934",wrong:["Only the deceased's estate may bring any civil claim — dependants have no independent right of action in English law to recover for their loss of financial support","Any person who suffers grief, distress or bereavement as a result of the death has an automatic right to bring a civil claim for compensatory damages","Dependants and the estate bring all their claims under a single unified statutory framework with no meaningful distinction between the two categories of claim"],e:"The Fatal Accidents Act 1976 allows dependants to claim for loss of dependency. The Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1934 lets the estate bring surviving claims. Both may operate concurrently."}
];

const TOPICS=[…new Set(QS.map(q=>q.t))];
let activeTopic=“all”,pool=[],idx=0,score=0,total=0,answered=false;
const hist={};TOPICS.forEach(t=>hist[t]={r:0,n:0});

function shuffle(a){let b=[…a];for(let i=b.length-1;i>0;i–){let j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(i+1));[b[i],b[j]]=[b[j],b[i]];}return b;}
function getPool(){return activeTopic===“all”?[…QS]:QS.filter(q=>q.t===activeTopic);}

function startQuiz(){
pool=shuffle(getPool()).map(q=>{
const opts=shuffle([{text:q.right,correct:true},…q.wrong.map(w=>({text:w,correct:false}))]);
return{…q,opts,correctIdx:opts.findIndex(o=>o.correct)};
});
idx=0;score=0;total=0;answered=false;
TOPICS.forEach(t=>{hist[t]={r:0,n:0};});
renderQ();
}

function buildStrip(){
const el=document.getElementById(‘ts’);
const all=[{id:“all”,label:“All topics”,n:QS.length},…TOPICS.map(t=>({id:t,label:t,n:QS.filter(q=>q.t===t).length}))];
el.innerHTML=all.map(c=>`<button class="chip${activeTopic===c.id?" on":""}" onclick="pickTopic('${c.id}')">${c.label} (${c.n})</button>`).join(’’);
}

function pickTopic(t){activeTopic=t;buildStrip();startQuiz();}
function updateScore(){document.getElementById(‘sc’).textContent=score+” / “+total;}

function renderQ(){
const m=document.getElementById(‘main’);
if(idx>=pool.length){renderResults();return;}
const q=pool[idx];
const pct=Math.round(idx/pool.length*100);
document.getElementById(‘pf’).style.width=pct+’%’;
document.getElementById(‘pt’).textContent=‘Question ‘+(idx+1)+’ of ‘+pool.length;
updateScore();
const L=[‘A’,‘B’,‘C’,‘D’];
m.innerHTML=’<div class="topic-tag">’+q.t+’</div><div class="question">’+q.q+’</div><div class="options" id="opts">’+
q.opts.map((o,i)=>’<button class="opt" id="o'+i+'" onclick="pick('+i+')"><span class="badge">’+L[i]+’</span><span>’+o.text+’</span></button>’).join(’’)+
‘</div><div class="expl" id="expl"></div><button class="next-btn" id="nb" onclick="nextQ()">’+(idx+1<pool.length?‘Next question \u2192’:‘See results’)+’</button>’;
}

function pick(chosen){
if(answered)return;
answered=true;total++;
const q=pool[idx];
const isRight=chosen===q.correctIdx;
if(isRight)score++;
hist[q.t].n++;
if(isRight)hist[q.t].r++;
updateScore();
document.querySelectorAll(’.opt’).forEach((b,i)=>{
b.disabled=true;
if(i===q.correctIdx&&i===chosen)b.classList.add(‘correct’);
else if(i===chosen)b.classList.add(‘wrong’);
else if(i===q.correctIdx)b.classList.add(‘reveal’);
});
const ex=document.getElementById(‘expl’);
ex.style.display=‘block’;
ex.innerHTML=’<strong>’+(isRight?’\u2713 Correct’:’\u2717 Incorrect’)+’</strong>’+q.e;
document.getElementById(‘nb’).style.display=‘block’;
}

function nextQ(){answered=false;idx++;renderQ();}

function renderResults(){
const pct=Math.round(total?score/total*100:0);
const msg=pct>=80?“Excellent \u2014 strong command of the material.”:pct>=60?“Good work \u2014 a few areas to tighten up.”:“Keep revising \u2014 you’ll get there.”;
const rows=TOPICS.filter(t=>hist[t].n>0).map(t=>{
const s=hist[t];const p=Math.round(s.n?s.r/s.n*100:0);
return ‘<div class="bd-card"><div class="bd-topic">’+t+’</div><div class="bd-score">’+s.r+’/’+s.n+’</div><div class="bd-bar"><div class="bd-fill" style="width:'+p+'%"></div></div></div>’;
}).join(’’);
document.getElementById(‘pf’).style.width=‘100%’;
document.getElementById(‘pt’).textContent=‘Complete’;
document.getElementById(‘main’).innerHTML=
‘<div class="results"><div class="res-pct">’+pct+’%</div><div class="res-label">’+msg+’</div>’+
‘<div class="res-sub">’+score+’ correct out of ‘+total+’ questions</div>’+
(rows?’<div class="breakdown">’+rows+’</div>’:’’)+
‘<div class="res-btns"><button class="next-btn" style="display:block" onclick="startQuiz()">Retake this set</button>’+
‘<button class="btn-out" onclick="pickTopic(\'all\')">Reset \u2014 all topics</button></div></div>’;
}

buildStrip();
startQuiz();
</script>

</body>
</html>